     I like Hairston's idea for reversing the Ctrl/normal keys, but I don't
think that'd solve the problem a whole lot.  I agree that #! is a better
shell command than !, which can be triggered by a miskey or line noise and
would be quite a pain.  However:
1) Since Ctrl-keys are inoperative on an IBM with the number_pad option,
attacking would be a real pain,
2) I simply do not like how the ctrl-keys move.  It tends to result in
sporadic movement which, while predictable, is not very intuitive, IMO.
     I think a better idea would be to make the following changes:
1. The ! command would toggle "attack mode" ("Attack is on/off").  With
attack mode off, keys would operate like normal except that a move to
attack would be like moving into a wall-- no response whatsoever.  Attacking
would be performed by either a *<dir>, -<dir>, or +<dir>, depending on
how whether any common keypads are missing any of those three.  I prefer
-<dir> myself.
2. Commands such as g, m, G, M, and -, when followed by something other
than a direction, should be interpreted as if those keys weren't pressed.
I'm awfully tired of "Unknown command: m,".  Nothing major, however.
3. Ctrl-keys can stay the way they are; I personally can't get much out
of them.
4. Shift-keys would be rewritten to stop at intersections in corridors,
but *not* at turns, diagonal passageways, etc.  This would be the toughest
part to code, but IMO, there is simply not a really good "long movement"
command in the game right now; either it stops so often with ctrl-keys
that it doesn't move much faster, or it doesn't stop enough, going right
by side-passages.  Again, Moria seems to have a good movement routine,
albeit the way dungeons are made may make for some necessary changes in it.
5. When blind, side-passages should be checked when using ctrl-move and
shift-move commands, if the player has them on the known map (obviously,
not if he doesn't).  Presently it moves to the end of a corridor and
stops (incidentally, this is actually a better movement mode, IMO, than
any of the others available, but it's not consistant) even if the corridor
makes a turn without forking.
6. When blind with telepathy, since one can / identify monster, "It hits!"
is an inappropriate message, IMO.  "The mukamil hits!" or "Demogorgon
thrashes you and pounds you to a pulp!", as if not blind, seems more
consistant with you knowing what's around you.
     These aren't all related to movement, esp. 2 and 6, but they do
represent long-standing gripes that weren't important enough to post
before.

